Linux represents an open file, pipe or socket using an integer called a file descriptor.
int socket_fd;
| Descriptor | Meaning         |
| ---------: | --------------- |
|        `0` | Standard input  |
|        `1` | Standard output |
|        `2` | Standard error  |


The same general operations apply to files and sockets:
read()
write()
close()

Example:
#include <unistd.h>

char buffer[1024];

ssize_t count = read(STDIN_FILENO, buffer, sizeof(buffer));

if (count > 0) {
    write(STDOUT_FILENO, buffer, (size_t)count);
}

